from collections import deque

def round_robin(jobs, quantum):
    queue = deque(jobs)
    t, finish = 0, {}
    while queue:
        name, left = queue.popleft()
        run = min(quantum, left)
        t += run
        if left > run:
            queue.append((name, left - run))
        else:
            finish[name] = t
    return finish

exercises.solution:
```python
from collections import deque

def round_robin(jobs, quantum):
    queue = deque(jobs)
    t, finish = 0, {}
    while queue:
        name, left = queue.popleft()
        run = min(quantum, left)
        t += run
        if left > run:
            queue.append((name, left - run))
        else:
            finish[name] = t
    return finish
```

The ready queue is a FIFO queue, which is exactly what a `deque` provides in $O(1)$ at both ends (see the lists, stacks and queues lesson). With a huge quantum every job finishes in its first turn, so round robin becomes first come, first served. With five 10 ms jobs and a 1 ms quantum, they all finish within the last 5 ms of the 50 ms total: excellent response time, poor turnaround for everyone, which is the trade-off from the lesson. A real kernel adds arrivals, blocking for I/O, priorities and the cost of each switch.

